243
00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:29.560
The first challenge unfortunately is security.

244
00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:32.240
So skills are actually executed by the agent.

245
00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:34.720
And they may or may not be nefarious.

246
00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.600
And so we're seeing a variety of risky skills come along.

247
00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:38.760
We're seeing malicious skills

248
00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:42.600
that are literally built to to cause harm in the open claw ecosystem.

249
00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.880
We've seen over 30% of skills to be malicious on it.

250
00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:47.840
It's a very easy way to hack in.

251
00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:49.880
We're seeing what we call negligence skills.

252
00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:52.360
So skills that really urge the user to do something but

253
00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:55.480
do not have any safety instructions in it, do not set any boundaries.

254
00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:59.960
So make sure you update the table correctly and there should be a line there

255
00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:04.760
like do not drop the table as famously happened in a few places.

256
00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:07.560
And so some of these safety instructions are lacking.

257
00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:11.280
And there could be vulnerable skills that guide for information that just

258
00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:12.520
sort of leaves you expose.

259
00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:16.160
The most common example of that is to use API tokens or other secrets

260
00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.440
as part of something that is visible in the logs, and might be available

261
00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.440
if it's available to the agent, it might be manipulated out.

262
00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.720
So security is a concern.

263
00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.040
And if security is a concern, governance becomes a concern.

264
00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:31.880
Because if some risks are some skills are risky

265
00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.920
then you have to ask well like which skills.

266
00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:39.120
Am I even using and do I know if they're risky and who's using which skill?

267
00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:42.400
And if one of them was malicious or was compromised,

268
00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:43.920
would I know that that happened?

269
00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.560
How do I track the track record?

270
00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:48.080
And so especially for organizations that are sort of mid-size and up,

271
00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:49.760
and really it should be for everyone

272
00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:53.400
some element of governance of just like know what's going on, some supply chain

273
00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:56.400
hygiene comes along and you have to do that.

274
00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:00.040
So security and governance is kind of one bucket.

275
00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:02.920
The next bucket of concern is around reuse and collaboration.

276
00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:07.200
And I love this story from the developer in sort of a large unicorn that has over

277
00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:11.760
1000 developers told me, which was just very exemplary of how things happened.

278
00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.840
So they had everybody loved skills and everybody was building their own skill.

279
00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.800
And a lot of people did the same skill again and again,

280
00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:21.280
whether it's related to the app or a code review or a test creation or something,

281
00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:22.760
many of us have the same ideas.

282
00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:24.640
And so they say, well, that's wasteful.

283
00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:26.360
Let's, let's create a shared repository.

284
00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:29.360
So they created a shared repository and everybody uploaded those skills

285
00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:31.400
or started uploading those skills in there.

286
00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:33.640
And very quickly it became a mess.

287
00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:37.600
And what happened was one, all these duplicates just went into the repo.

288
00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:40.080
But any new developer coming along and saying, oh,

289
00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:43.200
I don't want to write my own code review skill, I'll use one of those.

290
00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:45.920
There are seven over here. Which one should I choose?

291
00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:48.480
Like there's no indication of which one is good.

292
00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:51.160
They've seen a works on my machine problem, right?

293
00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:54.160
Where one developer is using one agent uploads a skill and it turns out

294
00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:58.480
that skill doesn't actually work well on another agent or collaboration.

295
00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:01.560
Problems with these PR that don't go anywhere because one person

296
00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:05.400
wrote a skill, another helpfully came and proposed the modification.

297
00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:10.160
How is the skill owner know whether this change is making it better or worse?

298
00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:11.960
Like there's no tools around it.

299
00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:14.960
And so nobody trusted anything in the repo.

300
00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.840
And eventually everybody came back to writing their own.

301
00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:19.640
So not very effective on it.

302
00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:23.800
And I think we'll come back to this, but I think big evidence kind of gap

303
00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:27.440
here is there was no quality control and indication of what is of good quality,

304
00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:29.680
and there was no management of the dependencies.

305
00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:32.160
And so

306
00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:35.720
security and governance, reuse and collaboration.

307
00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:37.880
And then the third bucket is really life cycle.

308
00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:42.080
And I think we've come to appreciate and know that software rots, right.

309
00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:42.640
If you write

310
00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:46.800
a piece of excellent software today and you do not maintain it, over time,

311
00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:50.640
it will stop working and eventually it will be harmful in your system.

312
00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.960
I think we've had enough mileage to know that the same is true for skills.

313
00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:54.840
You can create a skill.

314
00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:57.760
It works very well today and you don't touch it.

315
00:15:57.760 --> 00:15:59.920
And then over time the model's changed.

316
00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:05.440
The APIs around it change and might it might be incorrect.

317
00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:08.120
The infrastructure it's deployed and will change. Many things will change.

318
00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:11.600
I wrote three months here in the AI world it might be two weeks

319
00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:15.200
and your skill is already outdated and it rots and it becomes less effective

320
00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:19.000
and eventually harmful, actually guiding the agent to do the wrong thing.

321
00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:21.600
So we have to think about how do we maintain skills.

322
00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:23.800
Nobody likes maintenance, but we have to think about it.

323
00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:25.800
It is like a piece of software,

324
00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:28.160
and there's a bit of a carrot

325
00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.160
and stick going on over here, because maintenance is the stick.

326
00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:34.760
If you do not maintain, it's going to rot, it's going to cause damage.

327
00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:38.720
But now with agents, if you do maintain, you can actually turn maintenance

328
00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:40.040
into optimization.

329
00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:44.400
You can observe agent logs and pull requests and production logs

330
00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.640
and other information about what is actually happened,

331
00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:49.760
and automatically turn that into improvements

332
00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:53.720
and track that along into new skills, into modified skills, into removed skills.

333
00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:55.600
Always go to delete code, right.

334
00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:57.480
Just try to observe that.

335
00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.320
And so that's the carrot an opportunity and a risk.

336
00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:06.160
And both of them boil down to high quality autonomous agent powered maintenance.

337
00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:10.680
And so these are the three common change errors.

338
00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:14.120
There are like challenge areas that people are are feeling.

339
00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:14.760
And we're hearing it.

340
00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:17.760
It's actually amazingly consistent as we talk to different organizations

341
00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:20.080
about how they're adopting skills and agent dev.

342
00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:22.480
And the good news

343
00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:25.480
is they sound awfully familiar.

344
00:17:25.600 --> 00:17:29.720
All of those are problems that we have seen and addressed,

345
00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:32.720
and we've built amazing tools on how to deal with them

346
00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:35.920
in code, in software development.

347
00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:40.680
And so really what we need to do is we need to change our view around skills

348
00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:43.560
and not just think of them as like words that we move around.

349
00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:48.040
They're not like your your intranet docs, your sort of notion or confluence docs.

350
00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:50.000
They are like pieces of software.

351
00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.600
And I want to talk about five core kind of quality and software

352
00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:56.600
development tools that we need are on static analysis, dynamic

353
00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:59.520
tests, security tools, dependency management and observability.

354
00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:01.320
And talk about how can we apply those to skills,

355
00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.720
because I think those are the solutions for many problems.

356
00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.560
So let's start from static analysis.

357
00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:07.520
Static analysis

358
00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:11.840
is the exercise of inspecting code without actually running it.

359
00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:14.600
Right. And we've seen it evolve in depth.

360
00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:16.440
You can have simple enters that.

361
00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:20.640
Simply check if you're sort of styling is a certain path right.

362
00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:25.040
Or some typing things in non typed languages you can go into deep

363
00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:30.360
security analysis analyzers that inspect data flows within your

364
00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:33.600
your application and your code and find some deep problems.

365
00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:35.800
And today you have a generic reviews

366
00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:40.000
that can even look into the substance of what is the application doing.

367
00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:43.120
In all of those cases, it doesn't run the code, it just looks at it.

368
00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:44.600
And so it's an easy way to scale it.

369
00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:50.400
And typically one kind of measuring stick for all of your applications.

370
00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:54.480
So you will review the same way many many different types of changes.

371
00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:56.920
So this is directly applicable to skills.

372
00:18:56.920 --> 00:19:00.720
We can use things like linting Tessl has one version of that

373
00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:02.160
which is linked to just look to see.

374
00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:04.880
Does the skill have the right fields. Does it have the right forms.

375
00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:08.560
You can look at quality measures or security measures

376
00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:12.000
to be able to repeatedly find best practice flaws.

377
00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:12.400
Tessl.

378
00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:17.480
We use Tessl review to match a skill against the anthropic best practices.

379
00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:19.360
Does it have proper progressive disclosure?

380
00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:21.320
Does it have is it concise enough?

381
00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:23.880
Is it declare the activation well enough?

382
00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:25.560
And then of course we have the security analysis.

383
00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:26.680
We'll talk about that in a second.

384
00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:30.640
And then you can get into custom a generic review that actually looks

385
00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:34.360
at the substance of it, which is pretty much the same as the other ones.

386
00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:36.480
Again, at Tessl, we do that through the review.

387
00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:39.760
So static analysis is hopefully straightforward.

388
00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:42.720
And there's really no reason why we wouldn't do that. It is.

389
00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:46.440
It's just about the definition of of of stopping and defining what

390
00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:49.480
good looks like, what is correct functionality.

391
00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:54.080
The second world is tests and we all love tests.

392
00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.920
And you know, I think again with mileage a little bit,

393
00:19:57.960 --> 00:19:59.520
there are probably enough gray beards in the

394
00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:03.400
in the room that we will have experienced how it feels amazing

395
00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:05.960
to just ship something and not worry about tests,

396
00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:10.080
and then it feels not so amazing the day after as you try to modify it.

397
00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.200
And we've kind of come to learn, you know, in

398
00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:16.680
like visceral learnings, why we need tests to be able to scale.

399
00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:19.880
And you don't write tests for everything, but you have to have some tests.

400
00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:21.960
Otherwise you can't build upon what you've done.

401
00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:23.080
You can't develop.

402
00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:26.880
And because tests are test is a very big world.

403
00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:31.000
But all of tests, all the tests run some piece of your software

404
00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:34.000
and they scale from unit tests that are cheap and easy to run.

405
00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:35.320
So we run them in our CI

406
00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:39.400
and we build them out for a unit of software into integration tests

407
00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:41.000
that get a little bit into dependency world.

408
00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:42.920
And what happens for this piece of software?

409
00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.840
Not in isolation, but with a bunch of others.

410
00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:47.640
It's a bit more expensive to run, it's a bit harder to maintain and write,

411
00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:50.880
but it's useful all the way to end to end tests that are expensive.

412
00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:54.280
And they need a production like system and you run them, but they're very valuable.

413
00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:56.640
So we we invest in them proportionally

414
00:20:57.920 --> 00:20:58.720
in skills.

415
00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:00.320
The equivalent is evals.

416
00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:04.800
You take, you define an environment for some skill to run.

417
00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.960
You actually practically run an agent

418
00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:11.240
through the through a task in that environment.

419
00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.240
And then you judge the result.

420
00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:16.520
And you can do all of those in a variety of ways, just like tests, but

421
00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:20.160
you actually invoke it and you can do it in varying levels of depth.

422
00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:22.720
And I think what we're seeing is we're seeing at the bottom layer,

423
00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:24.280
we're seeing skill evals.

424
00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:25.720
Skill is a unit of software.

425
00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:28.800
So like unit tests, you're testing the skill in isolation.

426
00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.600
It's like a library that you're testing.

427
00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:32.720
Define what correct looks like.

428
00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:34.680
Build from there. Run an evaluation.

429
00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:35.600
You can do that

430
00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:38.960
only for the agents that you care about, or even with cheap open models,

431
00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:41.400
just to know that you're not regressing, just to know that

432
00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:43.920
however it is that worked before it still working.

433
00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:47.320
Now you can go into project evals

434
00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:51.360
that test a project, say a repo in with multiple skills, right?

435
00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:52.920
I have 20 skills installed.

436
00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.280
This is a more similarly

437
00:21:54.280 --> 00:21:58.520
like a better simulation of production of like a closer to reality world.

438
00:21:58.520 --> 00:22:04.200
And maybe I can take an old pull request and I can convert or extract a scenario

439
00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:05.240
out of that and load that

440
00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:09.760
with my sort of 20 skills and see again whether the judgment is,

441
00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.320
does it get a high score, right, what is the correct output of it.

442
00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:17.200
And so those are great to optimize project context, help you delete things

443
00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:21.560
and help you know whether it's useful or harmful to add a skill to your project.

444
00:22:21.560 --> 00:22:25.800
And then you can have more comprehensive test you can actually use

445
00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:30.760
in any of these evals to be able to run model evaluations on it.

446
00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:33.120
But if you really want a

447
00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:36.440
comprehensive test to be able to say, well, in my organization, in my develop,

448
00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:41.440
can I use GLM 5 or 2 six, which is much cheaper than opus?

449
00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:43.320
Or do I need to move to opus 48?

450
00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:48.400
Or maybe I continuously failed in this task and this new fancy model came along.

451
00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:49.880
Does it help me get past that?

452
00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:50.800
Then you create something

453
00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:54.160
that is more simulated, more extensive, like the end to end test.

454
00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:57.840
And these are not perfect analogies, but we can think about evals as tests

455
00:22:57.840 --> 00:22:59.360
and we can write them incrementally,

456
00:22:59.360 --> 00:23:01.600
but we have to get into the habit of writing them.

457
00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:06.040
And it's worth noting that just like tests scenario quality matters,

458
00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.640
we you can write tests that make you feel good

459
00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:12.200
because you get the coverage and they give you very little quality

460
00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:16.360
barometers like, I got the 100%, but, you know, it doesn't actually find bugs.

461
00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:19.360
And you can write a few tests that are super, super valuable.

462
00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:21.320
And you know that you can trust that framework.

463
00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:24.240
So you can change and you can run faster with more confidence.

464
00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:25.280
Same is true for scenarios.

465
00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:27.040
You can write some scenarios that are amazing

466
00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:30.680
quality parameters, and you can write some that are just a waste of time in tokens.

467
00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:32.440
So you have to think about that.

468
00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:35.000
Now I will say

469
00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.000
we're big believers in in in Tessl.

470
00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:41.440
Hopefully we've heard us ramble about this for for quite a while,

471
00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:44.800
and we have kind of good, good capabilities around it.

472
00:23:44.840 --> 00:23:47.440
We help you generate the test with scenario scenario,

473
00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.440
and we help you run them with the Tessl eval.

474
00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:54.080
And then one sort of side benefits, which we haven't really done

475
00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:57.600
well in software, is once you do static and dynamic analysis,

476
00:23:57.640 --> 00:24:01.360
you can actually aggregate a bit of this view and create a quality score

477
00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:04.400
so that when you're consuming a skill, like in that shared repo,

478
00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:09.000
you can actually figure out which one is is of high quality or not.

479
00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:11.920
And quality scores are always going to be imperfect,

480
00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:13.160
but they are useful.

481
00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:17.240
There are means of still knowing directionally this one is better than the other.

482
00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:21.600
So the third bucket is security testing.

483
00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.960
And it's actually very directly applicable to what we do in software.

484
00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:27.800
We can do static analysis. We should do static analysis.

485
00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:32.120
I was very happy to see all the apps players introduce

486
00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:35.400
skill scanning, sneak being one of them, but many of the others did as well.

487
